# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# EP FIPS posture reference image -- Rocky Linux 9.
#
# STATUS: REVIEWED REFERENCE MATERIAL, NOT A BUILT ARTIFACT.
# This file has never been built or run by the session that wrote it. No image
# was produced, no layer was pulled, no command below was executed anywhere.
# Every package name, file path, and module stream named here was read out of
# the Rocky Linux 9 repository metadata and the vendors' primary documentation
# (see README.md for the URL behind each one), which is a different and weaker
# thing than a green build. Treat it as a starting point to review, not as a
# recipe known to work.
#
# WHAT A SUCCESSFUL RUN OF THIS IMAGE WOULD EARN. One sentence:
#   "FIPS-based algorithms, with a validated-provider deployment mode."
# EP is not FIPS compliant and not FIPS validated. No EP package, receipt, or
# deployment carries a CMVP certificate. Nothing in this file changes that, and
# an image that builds cleanly is not evidence of any compliance position.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE HOST KERNEL QUESTION, ANSWERED HONESTLY UP FRONT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A container alone cannot fully enable FIPS mode. Red Hat states it directly
# for RHEL 9, which Rocky Linux 9 rebuilds:
#
#   "To enable the full set of cryptographic module self-checks mandated by the
#    Federal Information Processing Standard Publication 140 (FIPS mode), the
#    host system kernel must be running in FIPS mode. The podman utility
#    automatically enables FIPS mode on supported containers."
#
#   "The fips-mode-setup command does not work correctly in containers, and it
#    cannot be used to enable or check FIPS mode in this scenario."
#
#   Prerequisites: "The host system must be in FIPS mode."
#
#   -- Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9, Security hardening, section 3.4,
#      "Enabling FIPS mode in a container". Retrieved 2026-08-17.
#
# So: this image does NOT run fips-mode-setup, because that command does not
# work here. It installs the provider and wires the paths, and the FIPS state
# it ends up in is inherited from the host. On a host that is not in FIPS mode,
# this image runs and reports INACTIVE, which is an honest report and not a
# failure. Putting FIPS-related packages in an image proves nothing on its own.
#
# The host side is done once, on the host, and needs a reboot:
#   # fips-mode-setup --enable
#   # reboot
#   # fips-mode-setup --check      -> "FIPS mode is enabled."
# fips-mode-setup does what "update-crypto-policies --set FIPS" does and, on
# top of that, installs the FIPS dracut module through fips-finish-install,
# adds the fips=1 boot option to the kernel command line, and regenerates the
# initial RAM disk. That kernel-side work is the part a container cannot do for
# itself. (RHEL 9 Security hardening, sections 3.3 and 3.4.)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FROM docker.io/rockylinux/rockylinux:9

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The validated-provider side: OpenSSL and its FIPS provider
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# openssl-fips-provider ships exactly one shared object,
# /usr/lib64/ossl-modules/fips.so, and its package description is "the fips.so
# provider, a cryptography provider that follows FIPS requirements and provides
# FIPS approved algorithms". openssl-libs owns /etc/pki/tls/openssl.cnf and
# /etc/pki/tls/fips_local.cnf. crypto-policies-scripts owns
# /usr/bin/fips-mode-setup and /usr/bin/fips-finish-install. All four facts
# were read from the Rocky 9 BaseOS repository metadata, not assumed.
#
# DO NOT build the provider from source here, and do not update crypto
# libraries by any route other than the package manager. A certificate attaches
# to a specific build of a specific module on a specific platform; a locally
# compiled provider is outside it by construction.
RUN dnf -y install \
        openssl \
        openssl-libs \
        openssl-fips-provider \
        crypto-policies-scripts \
    && dnf clean all

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Node: it must be the DISTRO build, linked against the system OpenSSL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the step that decides whether the posture check can ever go active.
#
# Rocky 9 AppStream carries the nodejs module with streams 18, 20, 22, and 24.
# Its nodejs-libs package (which carries libnode.so, where the crypto lives for
# Node 22 and 24) declares ELF dependencies on libcrypto.so.3 and libssl.so.3.
# That is a dynamic link against the system OpenSSL, so the fips.so installed
# above is the provider this Node actually loads. Verified from the Rocky 9
# AppStream repository metadata. The Red Hat source spec for the same package
# configures the build with --shared-openssl, which is the upstream flag that
# produces that linkage.
#
# A Node from a nodejs.org tarball is the wrong choice here: those builds carry
# their own statically linked OpenSSL and no FIPS provider module, so the
# process has no validated provider to load no matter how the host is
# configured. Measured on this repository's development host (Node v26.5.0,
# statically linked OpenSSL 3.6.3): "node --force-fips" exits 1 before running
# any script, printing "OpenSSL error when trying to enable FIPS".
RUN dnf -y module enable nodejs:22 \
    && dnf -y install nodejs \
    && dnf clean all

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. OpenSSL configuration paths, baked in rather than left to the orchestrator
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both paths are Rocky 9 package-owned locations, not the generic ones in
# docs/deployment/FIPS-MODE.md. A missing environment variable must not be able
# to drop a deployment quietly out of FIPS mode, so these live in the image.
ENV OPENSSL_CONF=/etc/pki/tls/openssl.cnf \
    OPENSSL_MODULES=/usr/lib64/ossl-modules

# Note on fipsmodule.cnf. No Rocky 9 package ships one; it is generated, either
# by fips-finish-install on the host (which fips-mode-setup calls) or by hand:
#
#   openssl fipsinstall -out /etc/pki/tls/fipsmodule.cnf \
#     -module /usr/lib64/ossl-modules/fips.so
#
# That is deliberately NOT run here, for one reason that is solid and one that
# is not yet checked. Solid: fipsinstall runs the module self-tests and writes
# an integrity MAC on the machine that runs it, so baking it into an image
# records the BUILD machine's result rather than the runtime host's. Unchecked:
# exactly what podman puts in place when it "automatically enables FIPS mode on
# supported containers" was not traced to podman's source or documentation by
# the session that wrote this, so whether a container on a FIPS host needs a
# fipsmodule.cnf of its own is an open question to settle on your platform.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. The EP verify package and the posture check
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The repository layout is preserved and node_modules/@emilia-protocol/verify
# is linked, so the container exercises the real package subpath export
# ("@emilia-protocol/verify/fips-mode") rather than a relative path that only
# works inside a checkout. Build the package before building this image:
#   npm run build -w packages/verify
# Build context is the repository root:
#   podman build -f examples/fips-deployment/Containerfile.rocky9 -t ep-fips .
WORKDIR /opt/emilia
COPY packages/verify /opt/emilia/packages/verify
COPY examples/fips-deployment /opt/emilia/examples/fips-deployment
RUN mkdir -p /opt/emilia/node_modules/@emilia-protocol \
    && ln -s /opt/emilia/packages/verify /opt/emilia/node_modules/@emilia-protocol/verify

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Entrypoint
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default: report the posture. Exits 0 for PASS and for INACTIVE, 1 when the
# posture is self-contradictory (FIPS flag set, OpenSSL cannot digest) or
# indeterminate (the build cannot report a FIPS status at all).
#
# For production, add --force-fips so that a process which cannot load a FIPS
# provider refuses to start at all instead of running unnoticed outside one:
#
#   podman run --rm -e NODE_OPTIONS=--force-fips ep-fips
#
# --force-fips is accepted in NODE_OPTIONS (Node registers both FIPS flags with
# kAllowedInEnvvar), and on OpenSSL 3 it loads the provider named "fips" and
# fails process startup when that load returns null. Both facts were read from
# the Node v22 sources and the second was reproduced locally. --force-fips also
# removes crypto.setFips(false) as an in-process downgrade path, which
# --enable-fips does not.
ENTRYPOINT ["node", "/opt/emilia/examples/fips-deployment/posture-check.mjs"]
